Foreign-thread faults: a fault on a thread Wine didn't create hangs the process instead of ending it.
For ntdll of the CrossOver 26.3 engine SWTOR on Mac uses (athei's wine-build cx-26.3.0-8, Wine
11.0, dlls/ntdll/unix). Part of SWTOR on Mac, https://github.com/jdrly/swtor-on-mac.

segv_handler, trap_handler and fpe_handler (signal_x86_64.c) read the thread's TEB before
anything else: save_context reads the debug registers through amd64_thread_data(), and
trap_handler's handle_syscall_trap reads the syscall frame. On macOS Wine keeps each of its
threads' TEB at %gs:0x30. A thread Wine didn't create, such as a dispatch worker of a system
framework or Metal's com.Metal4.CompletionQueue, has 0 there, so the handler faults inside
itself and never returns. That thread spins on one core, whatever waits for it hangs, and the
fault is lost: no crash report, nothing in wine.log. Seen in this engine under Metal shader
validation on Metal 4, where Metal's validator faults on its completion queue (dttdrv/mullion
#165).

The guard runs in the three handlers before anything reads the TEB. Without a TEB it sets
the signal's default action, sends the signal to its own thread again (raise) and returns.
The signal stays blocked until the handler returns, then ends the process with that signal.
That also covers what wouldn't fault again on return: a breakpoint (the trap returns past its
int3) and a signal sent with kill(). Without the raise, such a thread would go on with the
default action left set for the whole process, and Wine's next SIGSEGV would end it. On a
thread with a TEB the guard reads %gs:0x30 and goes on as before, and a thread without one
never got past the first read of the TEB, so no path that worked changes. This is
OmniTroid/wine#8 (OmniTroid, https://github.com/OmniTroid/wine/pull/8, the fix in another
Wine), ported to these sources, with the raise added.

